Yes. There’s a whole class of these, but they’re not all equally controllable. A useful name is dual-access physiology: processes with automatic/default regulation that also expose some voluntary handle.

The interesting ones include:

* Breathing — automatic rhythm, unusually strong voluntary override. Change rate, depth, pauses, and exhalation. Useful for speech, swimming, exertion, singing, and indirectly shifting arousal.
* Blinking — automatic maintenance plus voluntary initiation/suppression. Useful for protecting the eye, visual timing, communication, and resetting visual attention.
* Swallowing — can be voluntarily initiated, after which much of the sequence becomes stereotyped/automatic. It’s almost a literal handoff from conscious command to an automated motor program.
* Gaze / pupils — gaze is highly controllable; pupil diameter is primarily autonomic and cannot ordinarily be commanded like a finger, but attention, light, accommodation, arousal, and imagery can influence it indirectly. That’s an important direct-control/indirect-control distinction.
* Facial expression — both deliberate and emotionally driven pathways contribute. Acting exploits exactly this overlap.
* Posture and muscle tone — enormous automatic stabilization happens underneath deliberate positioning. You specify something like stand there while lower-level systems continuously solve the implementation.
* Walking and other rhythmic movement — consciously initiated and steerable, while substantial moment-to-moment coordination can proceed automatically.
* Speech — perhaps one of the richest examples. Automatic respiratory and vocal machinery becomes voluntarily assembled into symbolic output.
* Attention — partly captured automatically by salience and partly deliberately redirected. You can’t perfectly command what enters awareness, but you can repeatedly reposition the attentional spotlight.
* Emotion/arousal — much less directly controllable than skeletal movement, but indirectly steerable through breathing, posture, expression, interpretation, attention, environment, movement, and social context.
* Memory retrieval — you can request a memory, but you don’t directly execute the search. You issue something closer to a query and wait for underlying processes to return candidates.

That last distinction gives us something powerful.

Command versus invitation

There seem to be at least three kinds of control:

\textbf{Direct:}\quad \text{intend} \rightarrow \text{action}

Move your finger.

\textbf{Triggered:}\quad \text{intend} \rightarrow \text{start program} \rightarrow \text{automatic completion}

Swallow.

\textbf{Indirect:}\quad
\text{change conditions} \rightarrow
\text{automatic system responds}

Calming arousal, inducing sleep, retrieving memories, changing aspects of emotional state.

That third category is enormous. Not being able to command a system doesn’t mean you can’t steer it.

You don’t order yourself to sleep. You create conditions under which the sleep machinery is more likely to take control.

You don’t directly specify pupil diameter. You manipulate variables upstream of it.

You don’t command yourself to suddenly find something funny. But context, interpretation, social cues, memory and expectation can move the system toward laughter.

That’s control through the system’s inputs rather than its output actuator.

And your acting observation fits beautifully

Acting can provide a low-pressure simulation chamber for state.

“I permanently am X now” creates a huge demand for consistency and proof.

“I’m going to behave as though X for the next ten minutes” is cheap and reversible.

That lets you sample:

\text{role}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}
\rightarrow
\text{body/environmental feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{learning}

Sometimes nothing sticks. Sometimes the performed behavior reveals that the supposedly foreign state was already within your behavioral range. Repetition can also make particular actions more practiced and accessible. But I’d distinguish that grounded mechanism from the stronger claim that pretending inevitably transforms identity.

Your phrase “modulate as needed” is actually the more sophisticated target.

Not:

Fake confidence until I permanently become confident.

But:

Learn how to recruit confidence when the situation benefits from it, then release it when another mode is better.

That’s repertoire rather than identity.

And now there’s a direct AI connection.

An AI system can also have processes that aren’t accessible through a straightforward command but are steerable by constructing the right context. Your guided-meditation idea effectively does this: rather than saying “produce insight”, you manipulate perspective, representations, comparisons, constraints, metaphors, and attention until different computation becomes likely.

That’s remarkably similar to biological indirect control:

\boxed{\text{Can't command the state?}}

\downarrow

\boxed{\text{Find what the state responds to.}}

\downarrow

\boxed{\text{Control those variables instead.}}

That’s a general-purpose operator.

Direct control is only one species of control. Steering, triggering, entraining, inhibiting, priming, scaffolding, and creating conditions are others.

